facts:
The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference when both stand on the same arc.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\draw[red, thick] (170:2) arc[start angle=170, end angle=250, radius=2];
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below right] at (O) {$O$};
\coordinate (A) at (170:2);
\coordinate (B) at (250:2);
\coordinate (C) at (10:2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\draw (O) -- (A);
\draw (O) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$2\theta$", angle radius=0.55cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--O--B};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=2.3] {angle = A--C--B};
\node[left] at (210:2.15) {arc $AB$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The angle in a semicircle is $90^{\circ}$ because the chord is a diameter, so the angle at the centre is $180^{\circ}$ and $180^{\circ} \div 2 = 90^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (O) {$O$};
\coordinate (A) at (-2,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0.845,1.813);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\coordinate (Ca) at ($(C)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (Cb) at ($(C)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\draw (Ca) -- ($(Ca)+(Cb)-(C)$) -- (Cb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Angles at the circumference standing on the same arc are equal, whatever point on that arc is used.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw[red, thick] (200:2) arc[start angle=200, end angle=340, radius=2];
\coordinate (A) at (200:2);
\coordinate (B) at (340:2);
\coordinate (C) at (110:2);
\coordinate (D) at (50:2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above left] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\draw (D) -- (A);
\draw (D) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.4cm, angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle = A--C--B};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.4cm, angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle = A--D--B};
\node[below] at (0,-2.1) {arc $AB$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

If the vertex lies on the minor arc, the angle stands on the major arc and the matching centre angle is reflex, greater than $180^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\draw[red, thick] (-20:2) arc[start angle=-20, end angle=200, radius=2];
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (O) {$O$};
\coordinate (A) at (200:2);
\coordinate (B) at (340:2);
\coordinate (C) at (270:2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below] at (C) {$C$};
\draw (O) -- (A);
\draw (O) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\draw (-20:0.6) arc[start angle=-20, end angle=200, radius=0.6];
\node at (0,1.0) {$2\theta$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--C--A};
\node[above] at (0,2.05) {major arc $AB$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

